To achieve this, you can use HTML and CSS to place the SVG icon on the right side of the input field. You can also add an `onClick` event handler to the SVG icon so it calls a JavaScript function when clicked. Here's a simple example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Input with SVG Icon</title>
    <style>
        .input-container {
            position: relative;
            display: inline-block;
        }

        .search-input {
            padding-right: 30px; /* space for the icon */
        }

        .search-icon {
            position: absolute;
            right: 5px;
            top: 50%;
            transform: translateY(-50%);
            cursor: pointer;
            width: 20px;
            height: 20px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="input-container">
        <input type="text" class="search-input" id="searchInput" placeholder="Search...">
        <svg class="search-icon" onclick="executeSearch()" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
            <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
        </svg>
    </div>

    <script>
        function executeSearch() {
            var inputValue = document.getElementById("searchInput").value;
            alert("You searched for: " + inputValue);
            // Add your search function logic here
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works:

1. **HTML Structure**: 
   - The `.input-container` div contains the input and the SVG icon.
   - The input field has extra padding on the right to accommodate the SVG icon.

2. **CSS**:
   - `.search-input`: Sets right padding to avoid text overlap with the icon.
   - `.search-icon`: Positioned absolutely within the container to the right and vertically centered. A cursor style of `pointer` indicates that it is clickable.

3. **JavaScript**:
   - The `executeSearch` function is called when the SVG icon is clicked. It gets the value from the input field and performs an action, such as displaying an alert. You can replace this logic with any search functionality you intend to implement.

This setup allows you to have a clickable search icon to the right of the input field that triggers a function to process the input.

